I have the full picture. Here is the detailed report.

## Verdict up front

The relay itself is a synchronous, unbuffered pass-through — **no queuing, no batching, no `setInterval`-based forwarding, no coalescing**. Per aim frame it does one `JSON.parse`, one field write, one `JSON.stringify`, one `ws.send`. On loopback that is tens of microseconds. **The relay is almost certainly not the source of the lag.** The two structural contributors it *does* own are the `cloudflared` quick tunnel on the phone leg and the complete absence of backpressure handling. Details below, plus four real bugs.

---

## 1. Message flow and the forwarding hot path

Entry point, `C:\Users\Ayden's mini\Desktop\GameDev\RockSkipping\relay\server.js:340-373`:

```js
340:  ws.on('message', (raw) => {
341:    if (isRateLimited(ws.meta)) return; // drop silently; a flooding client gets no feedback loop
342:
343:    let data;
344:    try {
345:      data = JSON.parse(raw);
346:    } catch {
349:      sendError(ws, 'BAD_JSON', 'Message was not valid JSON.');
350:      return;
351:    }
352:
353:    if (!data || typeof data !== 'object' || typeof data.type !== 'string') {
354:      sendError(ws, 'BAD_MESSAGE', 'Message must be an object with a "type".');
356:    }
357:
358:    switch (data.type) {
359:      case 'CREATE_ROOM':  handleCreateRoom(ws); return;
362:      case 'JOIN_ROOM':    handleJoinRoom(ws, data); return;
365:      case 'PING':         send(ws, { type: 'PONG' }); return;
368:      default:
369:        if (ws.meta.role === 'player') relayToHost(ws, data);
370:        else if (ws.meta.role === 'host') relayToPlayers(ws, data);
371:        else sendError(ws, 'NOT_REGISTERED', 'Send CREATE_ROOM or JOIN_ROOM first.');
372:    }
373:  });
```

`AIM` and `THROW` both fall through to `default`. Phone → game, `server.js:192-202`:

```js
192: function relayToHost(ws, data) {
193:   const room = rooms.get(ws.meta.roomCode);
194:   if (!room) { sendError(ws, 'NOT_IN_ROOM', 'You are not in a game.'); return; }
198:   room.lastActivity = Date.now();
199:   // Stamp the sender server-side so a phone cannot spoof another player.
200:   data.playerId = ws.meta.playerId;
201:   send(room.host, data);
202: }
```

Game → phones, `server.js:205-222`:

```js
213:   if (data.targetPlayerId) {
214:     const target = room.players.get(String(data.targetPlayerId).toUpperCase());
215:     if (target) send(target, data);
216:     return;
217:   }
219:   for (const [, playerWs] of room.players) {
220:     send(playerWs, data);
221:   }
```

And the single send primitive, `server.js:65-69`:

```js
65: function send(ws, payload) {
66:   if (ws && ws.readyState === WebSocket.OPEN) {
67:     ws.send(JSON.stringify(payload));
68:   }
69: }
```

Every hop is synchronous within the `message` event. Nothing is deferred to a timer. `Date.now()` per message (lines 198, 211, 93) is the only syscall-ish cost besides the send.

**Constants**, `server.js:22-43`:

| Line | Constant | Value |
|---|---|---|
| 22 | `PORT` | `env.PORT` or `3000` |
| 23 | `MAX_PLAYERS_PER_ROOM` | `8` |
| 24 | `MAX_MESSAGE_BYTES` | `4096` (→ `maxPayload`) |
| 25 | `HEARTBEAT_MS` | `30_000` |
| 26 | `ROOM_IDLE_TIMEOUT_MS` | `4 * 60 * 60 * 1000` |
| 27 | `ROOM_SWEEP_MS` | `5 * 60 * 1000` |
| 28 | `RATE_WINDOW_MS` | `1000` |
| 34 | `RATE_MAX_MESSAGES` | `180` |
| 38/39/43 | `CODE_LENGTH` / `MAX_PLAYER_ID_LENGTH` / `MAX_QR_DATA_LENGTH` | `4` / `16` / `512` |

**Rate limiting**, `server.js:91-100` — fixed window, not a token bucket, per connection:

```js
92: function isRateLimited(meta) {
93:   const now = Date.now();
94:   if (now - meta.rateWindowStart >= RATE_WINDOW_MS) {
95:     meta.rateWindowStart = now; meta.rateCount = 0;
96:   }
97:   meta.rateCount++;
98:   return meta.rateCount > RATE_MAX_MESSAGES;
99: }
```

At the actual AIM rate of 60 Hz (`controller/js/throw.js:229`, `aimHz = 60`) the 180 ceiling has 3x headroom, so **the limiter is not dropping aim frames**. The headroom was deliberately raised from 60 to 180 for exactly this reason (comment at `server.js:29-34`, and README lines 151-154). This is worth ruling out explicitly because drops here are silent.

The only timers in the process are the 30 s heartbeat (`server.js:399-409`) and the 5-minute room sweep (`server.js:413-420`). Neither touches message forwarding.

## 2. WebSocket library, options, HTTP server, static files

- **`ws` 8.21.1** (`relay/package-lock.json:24-27`), plus `qrcode-generator` 2.0.4. No `bufferutil` / `utf-8-validate` native addons installed, so masking and UTF-8 validation run in JS — irrelevant at these volumes.
- **Server construction, `server.js:324`:** `new WebSocketServer({ server: httpServer, maxPayload: MAX_MESSAGE_BYTES })`.
  - **`perMessageDeflate` is off.** The `WebSocketServer` default is `false` (`relay/node_modules/ws/lib/websocket-server.js:76`), and since the server never accepts the extension offer (`websocket-server.js:299-314`), no compression is negotiated even though browsers offer it. Good — this is the classic latency trap and it is not present.
  - `maxPayload: 4096` means an oversized frame **closes the connection with 1009**, it does not merely drop the message. AIM payloads are ~150 bytes, so no risk today, but it is a cliff rather than a slope.
  - `skipUTF8Validation` left at `false`; `autoPong` default `true`.
- **HTTP server, `server.js:306-322`:** plain `http.createServer`, routing `/health` → JSON, `/qr` → QR module matrix, everything else → static. TCP_NODELAY is inherited: Node's `http.Server` defaults `noDelay: true` (verified on this machine: `node v24.15.0 http noDelay default = true`), and the socket keeps that setting across the WebSocket upgrade, so **Nagle is not batching outbound frames**. It is not set explicitly though, so it is a silent dependency on a Node default.
- **Static serving, `server.js:245-264`:** `fs.readFile` per request, `Cache-Control: no-cache`, seven-entry MIME table (`server.js:235-243`). Serves `../controller` (`server.js:233`). Not on the hot path.

## 3. Room and player management

Rooms live in one `Map<code, {code, host, players: Map<playerId, ws>, lastActivity}>` (`server.js:45-46`). Codes are 4 chars from a 24-letter I/O-free alphabet, `crypto.randomInt`, 100 collision retries (`server.js:52-63`).

- **Host:** `CREATE_ROOM` → one room per connection, guarded by `ws.meta.role` (`server.js:117-137`).
- **Player:** `JOIN_ROOM` validates code and name, uppercases and truncates the name to 16 chars (`server.js:79-89`), then keys the player map **by sanitized display name** (`server.js:179`). Identity is the name — there is no token.
- **Reconnect** (`server.js:165-177`): an existing entry for the same name is treated as reclaiming the slot; the old socket has `meta.roomCode` nulled and is closed with 1000 before the new one is stored. That ordering, plus the `room.players.get(playerId) === ws` guard in the close handler (`server.js:387`), correctly prevents the late close event from evicting the new socket. This particular race is handled well.
- **Identity stamping** (`server.js:200`) overwrites any client-supplied `playerId` — but note it does *not* stamp `roomCode`, so a phone can put an arbitrary `roomCode` field in an AIM frame; the relay ignores it and routes by connection state, which is correct.
- **Close** (`server.js:375-392`): host close → `closeRoom`; player close → delete + `PLAYER_LEFT` to host.

## 4. Per-message overhead

| Cost | Where | Assessment |
|---|---|---|
| `JSON.parse` per inbound message | `server.js:345` | ~1-3 µs at this size. Unavoidable given routing needs `type`. |
| `JSON.stringify` per **recipient** | `server.js:67` | Broadcasts re-serialize the identical object once per player — `server.js:219-221`. With 8 players that is 8 identical stringifies per host frame. Real but small; hoisting one `JSON.stringify` above the loop and using `ws.send(str)` is the trivial fix. |
| Logging on hot path | none | `log()` (`server.js:75-77`, with a `new Date().toISOString()` per call) is only invoked on create/join/leave/close/error. **The forwarding path logs nothing.** Correct. |
| O(n) scans per message | none | Both directions are `Map.get` lookups. The only iteration is the broadcast fan-out, which is inherently O(players). |
| `Date.now()` per message | `server.js:93, 198, 211` | Three calls per forwarded message. Negligible. |

## 5. Bugs

**A. No backpressure handling — the one relay-side mechanism that actually produces growing lag.** `send()` (`server.js:65-69`) never inspects `ws.bufferedAmount` and never passes a completion callback. If a phone's downlink or the host socket stalls for a few hundred ms, frames accumulate in the `ws` sender queue and are delivered late, in order, forever behind. Because AIM is a 60 Hz stream of *strictly cosmetic, latest-value-wins* data, queued frames are worthless the moment a newer one exists — yet they are all delivered. Symptom: aim preview that lags and then "catches up" in a rush, worsening over a session rather than being constant. Fix shape: in `relayToHost`/`relayToPlayers`, drop the frame when `ws.bufferedAmount > ~64KB`, or keep only the newest AIM per player.

**B. `closeRoom` never tells the host, and permanently bricks it** (`server.js:102-111`):

```js
102: function closeRoom(room, reason) {
103:   for (const [, playerWs] of room.players) {
104:     send(playerWs, { type: 'ROOM_CLOSED', reason });
105:     if (playerWs.meta) playerWs.meta.roomCode = null;
106:     playerWs.close(1000, reason);
107:   }
108:   room.players.clear();
109:   rooms.delete(room.code);
```

Players are notified and reset; the host is not. After the idle sweep fires (`server.js:413-420`), the Unity host socket is still open with `meta.roomCode` pointing at a deleted room and `meta.role === 'host'`. Every subsequent broadcast hits `NOT_IN_ROOM` (`server.js:207`), and it cannot recover: `CREATE_ROOM` is rejected with `ALREADY_REGISTERED` (`server.js:118-121`), and `RelayClient` only sends `CREATE_ROOM` on connect (`Assets/Scripts/Net/RelayClient.cs:104`). The TV shows a dead room code with no path back short of restarting Unity. Needs 4 h idle to trigger, so it is a "left it running overnight" bug rather than a party bug.

**C. Slot/socket leak when one connection joins twice under different keys** (`server.js:139-189`). `handleJoinRoom` never removes the socket's *previous* registration before writing the new one. A connection that joins as `AJ` and then re-sends `JOIN_ROOM` with `BOB`, or with a different room code, leaves the `AJ` entry (and, for a room switch, the entry in the old room) pointing at a socket that will never clean it up — the close handler only deletes `ws.meta.playerId` in `ws.meta.roomCode` (`server.js:387-391`). Consequences: a permanently consumed slot toward `MAX_PLAYERS_PER_ROOM`, no `PLAYER_LEFT` for the ghost, and a stale roster on the TV. The shipped controller sends `JOIN_ROOM` with fixed credentials (`controller/js/net.js:76-84`), so this needs an unusual or hostile client — latent, not routine.

**D. Static path check is prefix-based, not boundary-based** (`server.js:248-254`): `resolved.startsWith(STATIC_DIR)` accepts a *sibling directory whose name starts with* `controller` — e.g. `controller-private`. Combined with `decodeURIComponent` on Windows (where `%5C` becomes a path separator), the guard is weaker than intended. Fix is `resolved === STATIC_DIR || resolved.startsWith(STATIC_DIR + path.sep)`. Not a lag issue.

**E. Minor:** `relayToPlayers` uppercases `targetPlayerId` (`server.js:214`) but does not apply the 16-char truncation `sanitizePlayerId` used when storing (`server.js:81`), so a long target name can never match. Also, `wss.on('close')` clearing the intervals (`server.js:422-425`) is dead in practice since `shutdown()` already clears them (`server.js:429-430`).

---

## Where the lag actually is

Path is asymmetric by design (`Assets/Scripts/Net/LocalServices.cs:40-43`): **Unity connects to `ws://localhost:3000` directly** (`RelayClient.cs:38`), so the host leg never touches the tunnel. The **phone leg goes phone → nearest Cloudflare edge → QUIC tunnel → local `cloudflared` → relay**, spawned as `tunnel --no-autoupdate --url http://localhost:{relayPort}` (`LocalServices.cs:243-245`). Even with the phone sitting next to the PC, every aim frame makes a round trip to a Cloudflare datacenter and back. That is tens of ms each way, on a free quick tunnel with no latency guarantees — and it is a plausible single explanation for the whole symptom. The project's own notes already point here: `relay/fly.toml:2` ("Pick a region near the players — relay latency is added to every input") and PLAN.md:623 ("Target under 100ms on the same network; if exceeded, suspect relay host region before code").

Ranked non-relay contributors on the same path, for whoever picks this up:

1. **cloudflared quick tunnel RTT** on the phone leg — dominant suspect, ~20-80 ms each way.
2. **`setInterval`-driven AIM sampling**, `controller/js/throw.js:285-297` — the frame sent is `this.motion.latest`, i.e. up to one interval (16.7 ms) stale, plus mobile timer jitter. It samples on a timer rather than emitting on the `devicemotion` event that produced the data.
3. **Unity drains inbound only in `Update`**, `RelayClient.cs:73-79` — adds up to one frame (16.7 ms at 60 fps, 8.3 at 120).
4. **`AimPreview.smoothTime = 0.035f`** (`Assets/Scripts/Game/AimPreview.cs:46`, applied at 152-155) — `SmoothDamp` trails its target by roughly that long by construction, so ~35 ms of the perceived lag is deliberate and tunable.
5. Relay processing: sub-millisecond.

Rough budget: ~17 (phone sampling) + tunnel RTT + ~0 (relay) + ~8-17 (Unity frame) + ~35 (smoothing) ≈ **60-70 ms of fixed local lag before the tunnel is counted at all**. If the user is seeing "significant" lag, the fastest discriminating test is to point `relayUrl` at the relay while a phone joins over plain LAN Wi-Fi through the relay's own HTTP server (accepting the loss of motion sensors on the desktop test seat), or to time `PING`/`PONG` (`server.js:365-366`) from the phone and compare against the same round trip on loopback — that isolates the tunnel from everything else in one measurement.
